[Music]  so for the past  15 years we've been hosting diversity  and leadership  it's taken a couple of different forms  it's had a couple of different names  over time  but the spirit has always been the same  it's always been about  how can we help people from all  backgrounds see themselves  in business school and more specifically  here at the gsb  this is an incredible place where we  hope to help  you achieve your dreams and so to kick  us off today  there we have a couple of really special  guests um and to start us  off is someone who uh we have the honor  of guiding us through these turbulent  times as a school  so i don't think he knows this but  within the first couple months of me  being here  he said something that has really shaped  the way i approach  education said  education is fundamentally about  encountering  difference education about encountering  difference  sit with that whether it's a new formula  maybe it's a framework  uh a new philosophy or even listening to  the lived experience of someone very  different from yourself  these are different ways to see the  world with new lenses and approach  things from new directions  this is where learning and growth comes  and that's bringing us to today  diversity and leadership is about  celebrating the different perspectives  that you're going to encounter  in your everyday life at the gsb and in  your career beyond  and so with that i turn it over to the  captain of our ship  someone who again has been really  influential on the way i view this work  the one the only dean of the stanford  graduate school of business  john levin thank you will  and um welcome everyone  to this event it's just such a pleasure  for us to get to host you  uh for uh for an entire the entire week  of diversity and leadership we've got so  many good things coming up this week so  i'm really excited for everyone who's  gonna who's gonna be part of this and  and for all the the faculty and the  students and the staff  uh who've participated to bring this  week together  as will said this week is really an  opportunity you know for  for all of you who are interested  thinking about an mba degree  to to hear from  our community about diversity inclusion  here at stanford in the workplace and  beyond and about its role  in mba education and  here at stanford we we set out several  years ago to make  diversity and equity inclusion a major  priority at the school  and and that's involved a whole bunch of  different efforts  to first increase representation in our  mba class and  in our other educational programs as  well and and on our faculty  second to make the school just generally  a more inclusive and welcoming  place and that you know that you know  incre in curriculum decisions  in our speakers in campus activities and  in the  culture of the school and and here i  think you're gonna you'll see it this  week but that you know that  in the culture was really building on  what was already a very strong sense of  belonging and community  that exists at stamford and and many  many  years and hundreds and thousands of  people who've been at the school have  have gone into  building that foundation and then third  the view that we wanted to have an  impact on business leadership  and business practices that extend well  beyond our campus  and we do that through programs for  alumni for companies for entrepreneurs  investors and through the work  and careers of our own students who come  to the school and then  graduate having you know absorbed and  uh the skills and the the thinking and  the at a can-do attitude that sort of  permeates this place  before they they go off to to do amazing  things in the world  this year and i think you're going to  hear a lot about it this week we've put  a  a really special focus on issues of  racial equity  we put out a action plan for racial i  could do this summer  that was the product of work by uh of  students and and faculty and staff and  hundreds of alumni  uh who after the events of last spring  and the black lives matter protests  really felt a need to  get engaged at stanford and to work  together  to see what we could do to help move the  country  forward from the difficult period we we  went through  last spring and many ways continue to go  through today  so we've been very focused on executing  that plan this year and i think you'll  get to hear  quite a lot about some of those efforts  over the over the coming week and i'm  really excited  about the progress we've already been  making  i'm just going to finish by saying that  as we've thought about  diversity and inclusion at the gsb  there's several principles that we've  tried to keep in mind and i thought i'd  just share them we'll actually already  alluded to one of them but i'm going to  mention three  the first is uh the first is a  conviction that  every successful leader today no matter  whether you're  here in the u.s or here in silicon  valley where we are or anywhere in the  country or anywhere around the world  it is just a mission critical skill  to be able to assemble and work with and  get the most out of  diverse talent and that's not an easy  skill  actually to to to master it's hard to  bring people together  from from different backgrounds and with  different views i mean just look at the  challenge of leading this country today  it's not so easy to bring people  together  but it is something that anyone who  wants to go on to be successful in a  leadership position  has to think about and has to try to get  better at and that's you know that's  hard work and that's when we  we really focus on a lot in in our  teaching here  and in the in the way we do management  education  the second principle which which will  already mentioned is  is an understanding that as will just  said  before that that education itself is  about encountering difference and here  you know as well said  education is about encountering  different people  different cultures and different ideas  and so and that is a sense in which  diversity itself  is just intrinsically tied up with  educational excellence  i think that's it's a really important  point to keep in mind sometimes people  express these things as if they were  intention  or they were counter points to one  another but actually they're not  they're intrinsically connected that  having different points of view  is the way we learn and so that's a  really important thing to keep in mind  at every educational institution we  don't want to ever lose sight  of that and then the last principle we  try to keep in mind is that  just a belief that the gsb and stanford  and those of us who are who are part of  this place  have a responsibility to help make the  world  a place of broader and more equal  opportunity  and that you know comes about through  individual efforts and through our  collective efforts and  i think those beliefs those principles  have been you know what  have really helped to bring our  community students  and faculty and staff and alumni  together  around diversity inclusion and i'm i'm  so excited that all of you are going to  get a chance this week to  to be part of it uh and to sort of see  some of our thinking and we're going to  get to learn from you  and you know hope we'll down the road  see some of you on our campuses as  students so welcome and thanks for being  here and  i hope the week is fantastic  okay now i have the really great  pleasure of introducing  the the high points of this session who  are our our two student association  presidents uh cario miguel and emily  clawkins  and i i won't give them long interest  i'll just say that um  carrie and emily are so they were  elected as uh as co-presidents of our  student association their nba twos  and they were elected in uh and took  office  in march of 2020 which was  uh a difficult time to ascend to a  leadership position  on a on a campus and  have been amazing they are they have  really  shown just truly extraordinary  student leadership in a way that i think  probably a few students have had the  opportunity to  to get to show because they've they've  had to lead the the student body  through the pandemic in a very difficult  period in time and just in every way  have modeled a wonderful  you know wonderful run for leadership i  i i'm going to be so sorry to lose them  at the end of this year  but i cannot wait to see what they  accomplish out in the world so they've  been  and they've been incredible champions  for diversity inclusion so i'm going to  turn it over to carrie and emily to  kick things off thank you so much dean  levin for those kind words  and welcome everyone i think it might be  helpful to kick this off by sharing with  you all how i see diversity coming to  life on campus  and a few things that emily and i are  looking forward to as co-presidents this  year  prior to coming to the gsb there were  moments where i chose to  sort of minimize or adapt my identity in  an effort to mimic  leadership models i had been exposed to  but one thing i've learned  over the course of my time at the gsb is  that you cannot separate the human  from the leader i am my authentic self  when i bring all my identities  being a black man being born in nigeria  attending a state school in texas having  a non-traditional pre-gsb career in oil  and gas just to name a few  this means that while i still frequently  enter rooms real and virtual  full of people who may not look like me  or identify with my lived experiences  the gsb provides me and so many others a  platform  to embrace our identities as part of how  we choose to lead  from experiential classes dedicated to  answering the question  why would anyone follow you to  collaboration with classmates from all  walks of life  i found that the gsb provides many  avenues for finding and showcasing the  diversity of thought  backgrounds and leadership styles of all  members of our community  the next point of our differences coming  together and being celebrated is truly  where the magic happens  both inside and outside of the classroom  but with that said  our goals as essay co-presidents and our  students have crystallized in the  context of  all that 2020 has brought for us  grounding us  in what truly matters most we're looking  forward to continuing to serve the gsb  community and collaborating with student  leaders and the administration to push  forward  some of those important initiatives dean  evan mentioned including  gsb's racial equity plan designing safe  hybrid learning environments  launching student and staff recognition  programs and also trying to reduce  financial burdens  just to name a few in closing as you go  through this week of programming  i would like you to reflect on the fact  that although many of the environments  we occupy  are not perfect there is power in  courageously stepping into  uncomfortable and uncharted spaces and  making them better than how we found  them  i urge each and every one of you to  bring your full selves  and to see your identities as your  superpowers  we hope that you will one day soon join  us in leaving a lasting legacy  of positive impact on the gsb community  and the world  welcome again and i'll hand it over to  emily  thanks gary uh it is a central joy and  also the most intimidating thing ever  to speak after carrie has already spoken  because he is so exceptional  um but i just wanted to say something  people talk about a lot  is that business school is an investment  in yourself and i think in this way  there's a lot of  hope and faith placed in this place gsb  to transform us to to empower us and to  deliver  on that promise to deliver on that value  i  would see this place as totally fueled  by optimism  and a willingness to improve and to be  changed and to grow  and in that way i think that the gsb is  one of the best classrooms in the world  to learn this skill set and to empower  yourself to engage with difference and  to bring the best out of diverse teams  exactly as dean levin said um and as  kerry has already mentioned  most of like our proudest moments as  students um here at the gsb for  a little over a year but also as people  who have been on this earth for  25 plus years have happened in the last  six months  i think the opportunity to build to have  hard conversations  and to shift how people experience the  gsb is like a central privilege of my  life and of carrie's life  and it's been phenomenally important for  us um  i also think a level of this that's  important is that in my view our  generation  joins uh companies and purchases from  companies  that they believe they feel a sense of  connection to based on how those  companies  express social missions and move through  what is an incredibly  tense and important time in the world  and because of that  i think there's really few skill sets  more important  than graduating from the gsb ready to  lead a company  in a way where you're prepared to issue  a press release about how you feel about  what happened to the summer  where you're prepared to signal to your  customers and your employees that you  care and that you see them and that you  find what they face important  and central to the way you're running  your company and so in my view being at  the gsb at the beginning of your  career is fabulous because the  difference between the good leaders that  we all  already are and the great leaders that  we hope to become  is emotional literacy and the ability to  celebrate diverse teams  and so i've been really excited to learn  that myself here and to be alongside  other peers who are deciding to do that  work too  and i'm incredibly excited that you guys  feel the same way um  so i'm honestly honored to be here it's  always great to be here with my partner  in crime carrie um and thank you will  for having us this is a really special  way to start the week  thank you all for joining us dean loving  carrie  emily thank you so much um i know y'all  have very busy days so we can go ahead  and take it from here  for everybody on this call this is an  invitation throughout the week we have  sessions  with faculty members with students with  admission staff  come with curiosity i encourage you to  definitely check out the stuff  that you're interested in but as folks  have been mentioning this is  about experiencing the world and  listening to the perspectives of those  around you  i'd highly encourage starting that  already this week and maybe checking out  a session or two  that might have not been on your radar  before this is a wonderful chance to  already get in that practice

